\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two pairs over a ring}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Use an ordered basis and its increasing dual top word
#{\omega}. For a rank-two form with matrix
#{\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&a\\-a&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)},
the [ring Pfaffian](fdip-00UR) is #{\pfaffian_\omega(b)=a}.
For a rank-#{4} form with matrix #{A}, it is
##{\pfaffian_\omega(b)=A_{12}A_{34}-A_{13}A_{24}+A_{14}A_{23}.}
The top divided coefficient selects one pair in rank two
and two disjoint pairs in rank #{4}. In the latter case
the three ordered words have signs #{+1,-1,+1}.
These are the same formulas as \ref{fdip-00MD}, evaluated
in #{k} without division.}

\p{Over #{\mathbb F_2}, the block sum of two forms with
matrix #{\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)}
has Pfaffian one. Its exterior element #{z_1z_2+z_3z_4}
has square zero, but its second divided coefficient is
#{z_1z_2z_3z_4}, as in \ref{fdip-00TG}.}
